πh/;QUÛOŒŸ~Øã¥ÎW§ªûç√wèe˚iX0{"rendered":{"html":"<p>The <code>gcsl</code> Rust crate exposes the language pipeline without requiring the\ncommand-line program. You can evaluate one source, retain an interactive\nsession, or load a workspace supplied by your application.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-the-boundary\">Choosing the boundary <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#choosing-the-boundary\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The repository separates four responsibilities:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>gcsl</code></td><td>Syntax, types, evaluation, formatting, and semantic indexing</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>gcsl-disk</code></td><td>Finding workspace roots and reading source trees</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>gcsl-lsp</code></td><td>Editor protocol, document state, and request handling</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>gcsl-cli</code></td><td>Command arguments, terminal interaction, and output</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p><code>gcsl::run</code> evaluates a <code>SourceFile</code> as a standalone program. A <code>Session</code>\nretains names and values across inputs. <code>workspace::Workspace</code> evaluates\nselected files after a workspace check. The lower-level lexer, parser,\nchecker, and index are available when you only need a particular stage.\nConsult the Rust API documentation for exact signatures and ownership.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stack-and-lifetime\">Stack and lifetime <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#stack-and-lifetime\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Run the language pipeline on a thread with at least <code>gcsl::STACK_SIZE</code>\nbytes of stack, as the CLI does. Its configured size is 64 MiB. Parsing,\nchecking, lowering, and evaluation share documented nesting limits; a host\nthread's default stack is not a substitute for the required reservation.</p>\n<p>Keep the source collection and diagnostic input numbering together.\nStandard-library files participate in the source list, so diagnostics can\npoint into a helper as well as into user text. The library returns\nwarnings with successful results instead of printing them.</p>\n<p>A workspace or session owns memoization state and temporary directories.\nDropping it performs normal temporary-directory cleanup. Do not return a\npath to such a directory and then destroy its owner before a consumer has\nused it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"effects-are-host-effects\">Effects are host effects <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#effects-are-host-effects\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The standard library is embedded in the binary. Its process and file\noperations still run with the embedding process's permissions and\nresources. The library does not impose a wall-clock limit or turn pure\nchecking into an execution sandbox. A host that accepts untrusted programs\nneeds an external isolation and resource policy.</p>\n<p>Use <code>gcsl-disk</code> when you want the CLI's workspace-discovery behavior.\nSupply sources yourself when your host has a different storage model;\nkeep the language's dependency and scope rules intact.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"choosing-the-boundary","title":"Choosing the boundary","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"stack-and-lifetime","title":"Stack and lifetime","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"effects-are-host-effects","title":"Effects are host effects","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":[]}